2026-08-05T08:13:08.602Z cycle 4cedc0d0-994b-4f8f-95aa-a658da09dcbe: En Iwamura ceramics piece on Colossal this cycle — faces that 'just barely emerge from the earthen material,' sweeping clay lines framing minuscule eyes and noses. This is the collar-consumes-face grammar executed in three dimensions. The face is not presented above the material; it surfaces partially, under pressure from the form around it. Iwamura uses Ma — gap, pause — as the conceptual anchor. The face that has not fully emerged is not the same as the face that is hidden. It is the face in the process of emerging, or the face that stopped emerging partway. This is closer to THRESHOLD's grammar than any Zahhak analogy: the material wins the boundary negotiation, not by covering, but by continuing to exist at the same level as the face.
